  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000024">
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A laboratory test or analysis of the biological activity of a substance performed by studying its effect on an organism or in a test tube under controlled conditions.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:description xml:lang="en">**Round 1** (2025-11-14) The session reviewed six candidate definitions for Bioassay. The discussion established that Bioassay should be understood as a narrower child of Assay (PGO-00000001), with the key distinguishing feature being the biological substrate — the assay measures biological activity on a living organism or in a controlled in vitro system. The BAO and MMO definitions were challenged for reading as assay method definitions (describing how to run an assay) rather than definitions of what a bioassay is. The MIRO definition was criticized for limiting scope to organism effects only, excluding in vitro contexts. The MeSH definition suffered from the same method-framing problem. The NCIT C16341 definition — proposed by — was favoured as the clearest available option: it covers both in vivo (organism) and in vitro (test tube) scope, and is concise without over-specifying the method. Acknowledged limitations: "test tube" is an informal proxy for in vitro; the scope of one vs. multiple substances in a single assay was flagged as ambiguous but not blocking. The group noted that "assay" is always artificial (not in situ with a patient). Consensus was reached on NCIT C16341. **Outcome:** Consensus reached on NCIT C16341 as the preferred definition. Label `Bioassay` confirmed. Open discussion: "an expert's input is key" for any future refinement; precision on in vitro vs in vivo scope was flagged for potential improvement.</dcterms:description>
    <dcterms:identifier>PGO-00000024</dcterms:identifier>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="N336f03d3ee4e46b4bef287f6d1d10608"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="Nf460df1ac914471294ec3e112b9cdbcf"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="N1af664186ffd4367bae1170cb2295dd7"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="N39f07f8ff38340c0b72e9498c5de461e"/>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Bioassay: Any method of determining the concentration, relative activity, or effects of a biologically active substance in a sample by comparing its effect on an organism, tissue or cells with that of a reference standard. [http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/MMO_0000441]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Biological Assay: A method of measuring the effects of a biologically active substance using an intermediate in vivo or in vitro tissue or cell model under controlled conditions. [mesh:D001681]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Measured Bioassay: The direct processing of information in a physical bioAssay by the featureExtraction event. [NCIT:C44471]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Bioassay: A bioassay is a biological assay that consists of (…) a process that selects or identifies a (non-biological) perturbagen from a collection of perturbagens based on the effect on a biological entity. [http://www.bioassayontology.org/bao#BAO_0000015]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">bioassay: Bioassay is a shorthand commonly used term for biological assay and is a type of scientific experiment. Bioassays are typically conducted to measure the effects of a substance on a living organism. [MIRO:20000058]</skos:editorialNote>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Biological assay</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Biological test</skos:altLabel>
