  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000006">
    <dcterms:identifier>PGO-00000006</dcterms:identifier>
    <skos:broadMatch rdf:resource="https://biolink.github.io/biolink-model/Site"/>
    <skos:broadMatch rdf:resource="https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q57951700"/>
    <skos:broadMatch rdf:resource="https://schema.org/MedicalOrganization"/>
    <skos:broadMatch rdf:resource="https://schema.org/Place"/>
    <dcterms:rights xml:lang="en">"NCI THESAURUS™ is released under CC BY 4.0. Produced by the Enterprise Vocabulary Services group, National Cancer Institute, Maryland, USA."</dcterms:rights>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A healthcare organization, an institution, a facility, a healthcare provider, or a part or a constituent of any of the above entities directly involved in conducting a particular clinical study.</skos:definition>
    <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://evsexplore.semantics.cancer.gov/evsexplore/concept/ncit/C70777"/>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Site: A place, setting, or context in which something is situated or to which something is, or may be, bound. [https://www.omg.org/spec/Commons/20250201/Facilities/Site]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Study Site: A facility in which study/protocol activities are conducted. [https://evsexplore.semantics.cancer.gov/evsexplore/concept/ncit/C80403]</skos:editorialNote>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-T-internal-record-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-MD-output-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <dcterms:description xml:lang="en">**Round 1** (2025-01-30) The session revealed significant conceptual complexity around the term "site", which spans anatomical, geographic, institutional, and procedural domains. Experts distinguished between a high-level generic concept and more context-specific subtypes. Key findings: **BFO_0000029 (Site)**: Philosophically rigorous but too abstract for practical PGO implementation. Some experts appreciated its generality; others considered it impractical. **OMG Site definition** ("place, setting, or context in which something is situated or to which something is, or may be, bound") was generally preferred by experts for its clarity and pragmatic scope — however, the IRI is unresolvable (issue #48), blocking immediate adoption. **NCIT_C70777 (Clinical Study Site)**: Strongly endorsed for representing real-world entities engaged in clinical research. The experts recommended a stratified approach: 1. A generic "Site" — pending a resolvable URI for the OMG definition. 2. "Clinical_Study_Site" (NCIT_C70777) — adopt for Phase 1. 3. Additional Phase 2 concepts: "Study_Site", "Anatomical_Site", "Binding_Site". **Outcome:** Further update required to confirm OMG IRI status and finalise the pragmatic recommendation. **Round 2** (2025-05-28) Follow-up confirmed that the OMG IRI issue (#48) could not be resolved: OMG IRIs are not currently resolvable, per input from Asiyah Yu Lin (Pistoia Alliance). As a consequence, the OMG "Site" definition cannot be used for PGO at this time. The pragmatic proposal for Phase 1: 1. Defer a generic "Site" definition until a resolvable and implementable URI is available. 2. Adopt "Clinical_Study_Site" with NCIT_C70777 as the Phase 1 core concept. 3. Flag additional Phase 2 sub-concepts for subsequent alignment. **Outcome:** Final recommendation confirmed. Submitted to Steering Group.</dcterms:description>
    <prov:endedAtTime rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2025-06-12</prov:endedAtTime>
