  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGOConceptScheme">
    <dcterms:title xml:lang="en">Pharma General Ontology (PGO)</dcterms:title>
    <dcterms:description xml:lang="en">The Pharma General Ontology (PGO) is a controlled vocabulary of core pharmaceutical R&amp;D concepts with approved definitions, preferred sources, examples and external mappings. This resource is developed by experts participating in the Pistoia Alliance PGO project.</dcterms:description>
    <owl:versionInfo>2.0</owl:versionInfo>
    <dcterms:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"/>
    <dcterms:rights xml:lang="en">Licensed under CC BY 4.0, except where a concept states another licence.</dcterms:rights>
    <dcterms:created rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2025-07-10</dcterms:created>
    <dcterms:modified rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-11-24</dcterms:modified>
    <dcterms:creator rdf:resource="https://www.pistoiaalliance.org/"/>
    <dcterms:publisher rdf:resource="https://www.pistoiaalliance.org/"/>
    <dcterms:language>en</dcterms:language>
    <dcterms:alternative>PGO</dcterms:alternative>
    <dcat:contactPoint rdf:resource="mailto:info@pistoiaalliance.org"/>
    <dcat:contactPoint rdf:resource="https://github.com/Pistoia-Alliance-Inc/Pistoia-Alliance-PGO/issues"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#ConceptScheme"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Ontology"/>
    <dcterms:identifier>PGO</dcterms:identifier>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Created by the Pharma General Ontology (PGO) project of the Pistoia Alliance, approved by the PGO Steering Group.</rdfs:comment>
    <owl:versionIRI rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/releases/2026-11-24/pgo-skos"/>
    <skos:hasTopConcept rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000000"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000029">
    <dcterms:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"/>
    <skos:broadMatch rdf:resource="https://www.wikidata.org/entity/Q1047579"/>
    <skos:broadMatch rdf:resource="http://hl7.org/fhir/goal"/>
    <skos:broadMatch rdf:resource="https://biolink.github.io/biolink-model/target_for"/>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A material entity, such as native (gene product) protein, protein complex, microorganism, DNA, etc., that physically interacts with a therapeutic or prophylactic drug (with some binding affinity) and where this physical interaction is (at least partially) the cause of a (detectable) clinical effect.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">molecular target</rdfs:label>
    <prov:wasAttributedTo>pgo_r_to_public_md.v1.10.0.py</prov:wasAttributedTo>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Target: A Material Entity that is the object of an Act of Targeting. Although people speak of targeting a process, what are in fact targeted are the material participants. Only material things can be targeted for action. [https://bioportal.bioontology.org/ontologies/SLSO?p=classes&amp;conceptid=https%3A%2F%2Fwww.commoncoreontologies.org%2Font00000544]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">target: The role of a molecular entity (protein or nucleic acid) that is the presumed subject of an assay and whose activity is being regulated by an entity with a perturbagen role in that assay. [https://bioportal.bioontology.org/ontologies/BAO?p=classes&amp;conceptid=http%3A%2F%2Fwww.bioassayontology.org%2Fbao%23BAO_0003064]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">protein target: A peptide, protein, or protein complex that is recognized by an antibody. [https://bioportal.bioontology.org/ontologies/ERO?p=classes&amp;conceptid=http%3A%2F%2Fpurl.obolibrary.org%2Fobo%2FERO_0001545]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Synonym 'Biological target': partial overlap</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Biological Target: A biological target is a biopolymer such as a protein or nucleic acid whose activity can be modified by an external stimulus. [https://bioportal.bioontology.org/ontologies/CANCO?p=classes&amp;conceptid=http%3A%2F%2Fchem.deri.ie%2Fgranatum%23BiologicalTarget]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Synonym 'Drug target': DTO label — broader overlap</skos:editorialNote>
    <dcterms:description xml:lang="en">**Round 1 *(Phase 1 session)*** (2025-04-10) The alignment meeting evaluated proposed, existing ontology and controlled vocabulary definitions for "target" in pharma research and early development (R&amp;ED). The group emphasised that "molecular target" is a more appropriate and precise term than the broader "target", particularly in R&amp;ED contexts. Multiple sources were reviewed: NCIT C16128 (NCI Thesaurus) — preferred despite lacking a formal definition and framing being limited to malignancies; BAO_0003064 — too specific to assay design; GO:0003674 (Gene Ontology) — strong definition of molecular function but limited to gene products, excludes DNA; DTO_00000004 (Drug Target Ontology) — appropriate for drug development but overly narrow for exploratory research. The group noted that molecular target appears to function more as a "role" than as a material entity — consistent with other PGO concepts such as Drug and Active Ingredient, which also carry role-based semantics. **Outcome:** Recommendation to SG: (1) Replace "target" with "molecular_target" as the label; (2) Provisionally adopt NCIT C16128; (3) Consider minting a new, role-aware definition in the future. **Round 2 *(Phase 2 session)*** (2025-09-04) The group reviewed the pool of candidate definitions following the SG's rejection of NCIT C16128 (Phase 1). Two viable candidates emerged: **Option 1 (preferred):** DTO_00000004 — "A material entity, such as native (gene product) protein, protein complex, microorganism, DNA, etc., that physically interacts with a therapeutic or prophylactic drug (with some binding affinity) and where this physical interaction is (at least partially) the cause of a (detectable) clinical effect." Favoured by (Darko Hric), Ammar Ammar, an expert, and for its good coverage of entity types and explicit causal mechanism. Giovanni flagged the label mismatch (Drug_Target vs. Molecular_Target). **Option 2 (alternative):** NCIT C16128 — retained as a fallback but considered less suitable given SG's prior rejection and oncology framing. Open issues raised but not fully resolved: (1) the DTO definition covers drug-interacting targets but not vaccine development contexts (e.g. antigen); (2) pre-pathological cell targets not clearly covered; (3) DTO maintenance concern (not updated since 2018, not in OLS). **Outcome:** Recommendation to SG: adopt DTO_00000004 as working definition, acknowledging limitations; request label alignment to "Molecular_Target". Open issues to be noted as pending future definition refinement.</dcterms:description>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Biological target</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Drug target</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Pharmacological target</skos:altLabel>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGOConceptScheme"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N19a8cb8f3c384c1da428600292168c23"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N50e16f89155b4ad8bdc29d40e75eb632"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N420fae8849ee4c79a8a335b15a7e4021"/>
