    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N2fde7ce3792f43e8996d59e3408038dd"/>
    <skos:example rdf:nodeID="N474bce20e47148e6ac5768250a7265ac"/>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">drug: A substance intended for use in the diagnosis, cure, mitigation, treatment, or prevention of disease [https://biolink.github.io/biolink-model/Drug/]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">drug: A chemical or biologic substance, used as a medical therapy, that has a physiological effect on an organism. [https://schema.org/Drug]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Pharmacologic Substance: Any natural, endogenously-derived, synthetic or semi-synthetic compound with pharmacologic activity. A pharmacologic substance has one or more specific mechanism(s) of action through which it exerts one or more effect(s) on the human or animal body. They can be used to potentially prevent, diagnose, treat or relieve symptoms of a disease. [https://evsexplore.semantics.cancer.gov/evsexplore/concept/ncit/C1909]</skos:editorialNote>
    <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">drug_product: A material entity containing at least one scattered molecular aggregate as part that is the bearer of an active ingredient role and that is itself the bearer of a clinical drug role. [http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/DRON_00000005]</skos:editorialNote>
    <dcterms:identifier>PGO-00000010</dcterms:identifier>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="Nd77074027e794aeaa6a0f2301ceb6c32"/>
    <ns1:preferredSource rdf:nodeID="N0349a6d167aa410495dd1a09930141c1"/>
    <prov:wasAttributedTo>pgo_r_to_public_md.v1.10.0.py</prov:wasAttributedTo>
    <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGO-00000000"/>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-MD-output-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <prov:used>2-PGO-templates/PGO-T-internal-record-template-v1.7.md</prov:used>
    <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/PGO/ontology/PGOConceptScheme"/>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">drug</skos:prefLabel>
    <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Any substance which when absorbed into a living organism may modify one or more of its functions. The term is generally accepted for a substance taken for a therapeutic purpose, but is also commonly used for abused substances.</skos:definition>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">drug</rdfs:label>
    <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">drug product</skos:altLabel>
    <dcterms:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"/>
    <skos:closeMatch rdf:resource="https://spec.pistoiaalliance.org/idmp/ontology/ISO11615-MedicinalProducts/PharmaceuticalProduct/PharmaceuticalProduct"/>
    <skos:closeMatch rdf:resource="https://schema.org/Drug"/>
    <dcterms:source rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CHEBI_23888"/>
    <prov:endedAtTime rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2025-06-12</prov:endedAtTime>
    <dcterms:description xml:lang="en">**Round 1** (2025-02-27) The session explored the complex and context-dependent nature of "drug" as a concept in pharma R&amp;ED. Key discussion points: The DRON_00000005 (drug_product) definition was deemed unsatisfactory — it describes a product, not the drug itself. The group favoured BioLink_Drug and CHEBI_23888, both offering practical balance between generality and specificity. "Drug" may have enhancing properties, not just curative. The term "drug" changes meaning across pipeline stages (R&amp;D, development, post-market). PGO core concepts "compound" and "product" are closely related; maintaining all three as distinct concepts was endorsed. Three options discussed: (a) adopt CHEBI_23888; (b) use "pharmacologic substance" as the core concept label with "drug" as synonym; (c) propose an amended definition to the ChEBI team. **Outcome:** Preliminary preference for CHEBI_23888. Follow-up confirmation session scheduled. **Round 2** (2025-05-29) The session confirmed the Round 1 recommendation. Key conclusions: Keep "drug" as the concept label (along with "substance", "compound", "product") — as imprecise as they are, these terms are widely used in R&amp;ED. CHEBI_23888 confirmed as the recommended definition. No change to the proposed recommendation from Round 1. **Outcome:** Consensus confirmed on CHEBI_23888. Recommendation submitted to Steering Group.</dcterms:description>
    <dcterms:rights xml:lang="en">"ChEBI data is available under Creative Commons CC BY 4.0 licence. © European Bioinformatics Institute, EMBL-EBI."</dcterms:rights>
    <prov:generatedAtTime rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-11-24</prov:generatedAtTime>
  </rdf:Description>
